{"name":"Walnut Wood","hex":"#774e37","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"walnut","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hardwood tree of the genus Juglans.","A nut of the walnut tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a dark brown colour, the colour of walnut wood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/walnut"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Juglans","blocks":[{"text":"Walnut trees are any species of tree in the plant genus Juglans, the type genus of the family Juglandaceae, the seeds of which are referred to as walnuts. All species are deciduous trees, 10–40 metres (33–131 ft) tall, with pinnate leaves 200–900 millimetres (7.9–35.4 in), with 5–25 leaflets; the shoots have chambered pith, a character shared with the wingnuts (Pterocarya), but not the hickories (Carya) in the same family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Juglans_major_Morton.jpg/330px-Juglans_major_Morton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juglans","wikidata":"Q2453469"},{"title":"Walnut","blocks":[{"text":"A walnut is the edible seed of any tree of the genus Juglans, most often the Persian or English walnut, Juglans regia. They are accessory fruit because the outer covering of the fruit is technically an involucre and thus not morphologically part of the carpel; this means it cannot be a drupe but is instead a drupe-like nut."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Walnuts_-_whole_and_open_with_halved_kernel.jpg/330px-Walnuts_-_whole_and_open_with_halved_kernel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walnut","wikidata":"Q208021"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_%285653812987%29.jpg/500px-Baklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_%285653812987%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_%285653812987%29.jpg/1280px-Baklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_%285653812987%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_%285653812987%29.jpg/500px-Baklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_%285653812987%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Walnut is one of the main ingredients of Baklava and Turkish cuisine.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaklava_in_Bodrum%2C_Turkey_(5653812987).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Walnuts_by_RustedStrings.jpg/500px-Walnuts_by_RustedStrings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars